  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Graphs of linear functions",
  "summary": "Plot and interpret straight-line graphs using points, intercepts, equations and intersections.",
  "outcomes": [
    "6.4"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "n6-lesson-03"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 50,
  "objectives": [
    "Generate points for a straight-line graph from its equation.",
    "Find and interpret x- and y-intercepts.",
    "Find equations and intersections of straight-line graphs."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "n6-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-graphs-method",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Generate accurate points for a straight-line graph",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A point lies on a graph only if its coordinates satisfy the equation. Choose several convenient x-values, calculate the matching y-values, plot the ordered pairs using the axis scales, and draw one straight line through them. Two points determine a mathematical line, while a third point provides a useful accuracy check."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use a ruler or a digital straight-line tool rather than joining points with separate short segments. Extend only across the shown or meaningful domain and label the line with its equation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-graph-intercepts",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Find and interpret intercepts",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The y-intercept is where the graph crosses the y-axis, so x = 0. The x-intercept is where the graph crosses the x-axis, so y = 0. An intercept is a complete coordinate, not just one number."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "\\text{x-intercept: set }y=0,\\qquad \\text{y-intercept: set }x=0"
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "n6-lesson-04-linear-graph"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For y = -2x + 8, the diagram shows the two intercepts (0, 8) and (4, 0). Substituting either coordinate into the equation gives a true statement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-graph-intersections",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Interpret the intersection of two graphs",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Two graphs intersect at an ordered pair that satisfies both equations. On a drawn graph, read both coordinates carefully from the scales. Algebraically, set the two expressions for y equal, solve for x, and substitute to find the shared y-value."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "y=f(x),\\ y=g(x)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad f(x)=g(x)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A graph gives an estimate when the intersection falls between grid lines; algebra gives an exact value when the equations are known. In a context, interpret both coordinates and reject intersections outside the meaningful domain."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-graphs-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: find both coordinate-axis intercepts",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For y = -2x + 8, find both coordinate-axis intercepts and use them to describe how to draw the graph."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Set x = 0 for the y-intercept."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "y=-2(0)+8=8\\Rightarrow(0,8)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Set y = 0 for the x-intercept."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "0=-2x+8\\Rightarrow x=4\\Rightarrow(4,0)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Plot the two points and draw the straight line through them."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "(0,8)\\text{ and }(4,0)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Both points satisfy y = -2x + 8. The graph falls from left to right, which agrees with the negative coefficient of x."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-graph-intersection-example",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: solve a graph intersection",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Find the intersection of y = 2x + 1 and y = -x + 7."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "At the intersection, both expressions have the same y-value."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "2x+1=-x+7"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Solve for x."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "3x=6\\Rightarrow x=2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Substitute into either equation to find y."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "y=2(2)+1=5\\Rightarrow(2,5)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The point checks in the second equation because -2 + 7 = 5, so (2, 5) lies on both lines."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-graphs-recall",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Find the x-coordinate where y = 3x - 12 crosses the x-axis."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "4",
        "canonical_latex": "4",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At the x-axis y = 0, so 3x - 12 = 0 and x = 4."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-lesson-04-summary",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Method checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Calculate and plot ordered pairs using the marked scales, use an additional point to check accuracy, and draw one labelled straight line through the valid domain."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Set x = 0 for the y-intercept and y = 0 for the x-intercept. At an intersection, equate the two expressions for y, find the shared point and verify it in both equations."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
